<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John de Britto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1647">1647</a>–<a href="/years/1693">1693</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as João de Brito</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John de Britto was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> Mission of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Adopting the dress and way of life of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> ascetic, he made many converts in the Marava country of Ramnad. After one convert, a local chief, dismissed his additional wives, the Sethupathi ruler Kizhavan had him beheaded at Oriyur in 1693. He was canonised in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-de-britto.jpg" alt="John de Britto" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cleetuscls · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._John_De_Britto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>João de Brito was born on 1 March 1647 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> into a powerful aristocratic Portuguese family. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> de Britto Pereira, died while serving as Viceroy of the Portuguese colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1662 and studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. Before reaching the mission field he spent seven months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> completing his theological studies at St Paul's College in Old Goa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He travelled to the Madurai Mission in 1673 and preached in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> country, taking the Tamil name Arul Anandar. The mission was an attempt to build an Indian Catholic Church relatively free of European cultural domination, following the method of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberto-de-nobili">Roberto de Nobili</a>. Britto learned the local languages, went about in yellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and lived as a Tamil sannyasi, eating only legumes, fruits and herbs and taking no wine, and he tried to teach the faith in concepts that made sense to his hearers.</p><p>The ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> country imprisoned him in 1684. Expelled, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1687 and worked as a procurator of the missions. King Pedro II wanted him to stay in Portugal, but in 1690 he went back to the Maravar country with 24 new missionaries. Indian Catholics often call him the Portuguese St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a>, and he is also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The convert whose marriage led to his death was Thadiyathevan, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a> prince who, as a Christian, had to keep only one wife. One of the women he sent away was a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizhavan-sethupathi">Raghunatha Kilavan Sethupathi</a>, the King of Ramnad, who then launched a general persecution of Christians. Britto and his catechists were seized and taken to Ramnad, and from there to Orur, some 30 miles north along the coast, where he was executed on 4 February 1693.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> beatified him on 21 August 1853, and Pope Pius XII canonised him on 22 June 1947; his feast day is 4 February. At Orur a shrine built in Portuguese style stands beside the red sand dune believed to have been stained by his blood, and pilgrims from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims as well as Christians, gather there for its festival. Churches and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> schools bear his name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, Malaysia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John de Britto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kizhavan Sethupathi persecuted Jesuit converts and executed the missionary <b>John de Britto</b> in 1693.</blockquote><a href="/state/ramnad-kingdom">Ramnad Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Oriyur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1676" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John de Britto"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1676">What was here in 1676? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
